.TH TPM2SH 1 "2025-11-22" "tpm2sh 0.15.23" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tpm2sh \- a command-line tool for accessing TPM 2.0 devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tpm2sh
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fICOMMAND-OPTIONS\fR] [\fIARGUMENTS\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tpm2sh
is a command-line tool for accessing TPM 2.0 devices.
.P
.B tpm2sh
provides a virtual memory manager for sharing transient TPM
resources across multiple commands.
.P
Transient handles are cached in a per-user cache directory. The cache
automatically invalidates and resets on reboot, and does not require
attention from the user.
.P
.B tpm2sh
provides subcommands for creating and loading DER or PEM encoded keys in TPMKey
ASN.1 format.
.P
Several commands, such as
.BR create ,
.BR create-primary ,
and
.BR convert ,
accept a
.BR --policy " option to specify an inline authorization policy expression."

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -d, --device=\fI<DEVICE>\fR
Device file [default: /dev/tpmrm0].
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print help.
.TP
.B -V, --version
Print version.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B algorithm
Lists available algorithms supported by the chip.
.TP
.B cache
Lists cached TPM objects.
.TP
.B convert
Convert external keys to TPM keys.
.TP
.B create
Creates a secondary key or a sealed data object.
.TP
.B create-primary
Creates a new primary key in a specified hierarchy.
.TP
.B delete
Deletes active and cached objects.
.TP
.B evict
Create persistent object from transient object.
.TP
.B help
Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s).
.TP
.B load
Loads a PEM or DER TPMKey file to cache. If the TPMKey file does not include
a parent public area, a parent handle must be supplied with
.BR -P " or " --parent .
.TP
.B memory
Lists objects inside TPM memory or inspects a single handle.
.TP
.B pcr-event
Extends a PCR with an event.
.TP
.B reset-lock
Resets the dictionary attack lockout counter.
.TP
.B return-code
Prints a TPM return code in human-readable format.
.TP
.B unseal
Retrieves data from a sealed data object.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TPM2SH_CACHE_PATH
Overrides the default per-user cache directory used to store virtual TPM
state. When unset, a system-appropriate per-user cache directory is used.

